#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148288} :activity/id #uuid "ddf081b5-4271-58f1-ac92-6ac95f2e57d1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048622 :topic/id #uuid "af6ffcd9-235a-539a-acab-3fa95c86d5a2" :topic/math-academy-id 2526} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148747 :tutorial/content "A harmonic oscillator is said to be **forced** if it is subject to a nonzero external force $f(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny'' + by' + \\omega^2 y = f(t)\n$$\n\nIf the external force $f(t)$ is periodic and its frequency is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, then the system experiences **resonance** and the amplitude of the oscillations increases without bound over time.\n\nFor example, consider the following differential equation which represents a harmonic oscillator subject to a forcing function $\\cos(2t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny''+4y=\\cos(2t), \\qquad y(0) =0, \\qquad y'(0) = 0\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution $y_c$ here represents simple harmonic motion,\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos(2 t) + B\\sin(2 t).\n$$\n\nThe forcing function $f(t) = \\cos(2t)$ has the same frequency as the complementary solution above (both have a frequency of $\\Omega = 2 \\, \\textrm{s}^{-1}$), so the system experiences *resonance*. As a result,  the particular solution\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{t}{4}\\sin(2t)\n$$\n\nhas an amplitude that is proportional to $t,$ which means that the amplitude will increase over time.\n\nThe general solution in this case is\n\n$$\ny(t) = A\\cos(2 t) + B\\sin(2 t) +\\dfrac{t}{4}\\sin(2t),\n$$\n\nand taking into account the initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\dfrac{t}{4}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nNote that the amplitude of his function grows linearly. The graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/90/9032f93f083a100b14f732ba114e306fd0c9c5e0576ce218c9248c0e140854de.png)\n\n**Important:** Not all forced oscillators experience resonance. In the above example, if we change the forcing function to $f(t) = \\cos(t),$ resulting in the equation\n\n$$\ny''+4y=\\cos(t), \\qquad y(0) =0, \\qquad y'(0) = 0,\n$$\n\n then the amplitude of the particular solution is constant:\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\cos(t)\n$$\n\nThere is no resonance in this case since the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\cos(t)$ does not coincide with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\Omega = 2.$\n\nThe general solution in this case is\n\n$$\ny(t) = A\\cos(2 t) + B\\sin(2 t) +\\dfrac{1}{3}\\cos(t),\n$$\n\nand taking into account the initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(\\cos(t)-\\cos(2t)\\right).\n$$\n\nAs we can see in the graph below, the amplitude of the solution does not grow.\n\n![](images/0e/0eac5b659d3ea9712a3672eb443227d7c8cee5377ff52d7646ca263dc5e11007.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "77b16ea3-5ad1-5108-9c77-d314e6e9ddf3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4069 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e32b8ecd-fee2-56e7-867d-2ac1f9f9b872" :step/math-academy-id 24279 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148307}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147448 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147376 :question/id #uuid "bd1d2ef2-9472-5e57-b071-fbbaf80023da" :question/math-academy-id "e-6731"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29e7ead-5eae-5769-9502-7953d7018782" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048291}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434366}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Forced Harmonic Motion"} :step/id #uuid "39e8e7e0-57a8-511a-aef2-6b002039cb19" :step/math-academy-id 24721 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148317}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147449 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147377 :question/id #uuid "11a44451-5b9f-5892-b321-925b2a393551" :question/math-academy-id "e-6732"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434369}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"} :step/id #uuid "0dd1a25e-e81d-5bf2-9e52-1d14c32c9689" :step/math-academy-id 24722 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148318}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148309 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147378 :question/id #uuid "aa77f4ef-67b6-5041-9a3b-1a36ffd5582d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6733"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050805}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434368}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"} :step/id #uuid "f42f709a-7c4f-5416-bf43-29a32c645bd5" :step/math-academy-id 24723 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147451 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147379 :question/id #uuid "20b53dff-43b9-5256-ab7d-67aff56db408" :question/math-academy-id "e-6734"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5266e491-2718-56a9-b5a7-f4a86a8a01c7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049099}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445742}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position of a Particle Oscillating With Forced Harmonic Motion at a Moment"} :step/id #uuid "dbf9225d-b92c-5540-96eb-190970598cab" :step/math-academy-id 24724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148766 :tutorial/content "Remember that, if an object of mass $m$ is attached to a spring with spring constant $k,$ and the damping coefficient is $c,$ then the equation of motion is\n\n$$\nmy'' = -cy'-ky.\n$$\n\nIf there is an external force $F(t),$ then the equation of motion is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y + F(t) .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ab390ee6-c457-55d4-a265-9d62a1955ab8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4169 :tutorial/title "The Equation of Motion for a Forced Spring-Mass System"} :step/id #uuid "62673a11-379c-5680-872b-45a2328762ae" :step/math-academy-id 25340 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148308}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148318 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148767 :tutorial/content "Given a forced harmonic motion equation *without* damping,\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = f(t),\n$$\n\nthe complementary solution can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nAlternatively, given a forced harmonic motion equation *with* damping,\n\n$$\ny'' + by' + \\omega^2 y = f(t),\n$$\n\nif the roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = p \\pm \\textrm{i}q,$ then the complementary solution can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y_c = R e^{p t} \\cos{(q t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y_c = R e^{p t} \\sin{(q t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y_c =e^{p t} \\left( A\\cos{q t} + B\\sin{q t} \\right)$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b4683094-19f8-557e-b0c7-7aad98cab72c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4170 :tutorial/title "General Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"} :step/id #uuid "4be5106f-eac3-59d0-8ca0-5316fe821456" :step/math-academy-id 25341 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148309}}] :activity/title "Forced Oscillators" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147705}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445747} :activity/id #uuid "5802b41f-ba3b-55d7-ba60-f9644f140c8f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048622 :topic/id #uuid "af6ffcd9-235a-539a-acab-3fa95c86d5a2" :topic/math-academy-id 2526} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445742 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2637def5-3485-53ec-a92f-f5dafce114a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(2)=(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡6}{12}+\\frac{1}{2})\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445729} {:answer/id #uuid "3aa62a3b-1769-5d05-86db-8812b1d7f0d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(2)=(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡12}{12}+1)\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445732} {:answer/id #uuid "41ac66cb-2440-5faa-9ffe-032ed8000f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(2)=(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡6}{6}+2)\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445733} {:answer/id #uuid "4e1b4f6f-1427-536a-99b9-724dc4e64da2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(2)=(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡12}{6}+1)\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445734} {:answer/id #uuid "c8676819-95da-5da8-9760-80284b82e282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(2)=(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡12}{6}-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445736}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445732} :answer-field/id #uuid "97ee9863-7827-5ffb-961e-95840bc8f69d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f3e9f427-a3b5-5de3-bad3-b21c91d9d341" :question/math-academy-id "q-94309" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in centimeters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+36y=18t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $1\\,\\text{cm/s},$ find the position of the particle after $2$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=36\\,⟹\\,\\omega =6.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is a linear polynomial. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a linear polynomial, i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha +\\beta t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,\\beta $ are constants to be determined.\n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =0,\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{t}{2},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t+\\frac{t}{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with velocity $1\\,\\text{cm/s}$, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=1.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=0,\\,B=\\frac{1}{12}\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t}{12}+\\frac{t}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $2$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡6(2)}{12}+\\frac{(2)}{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡12}{12}+1)\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5a511ebc-694e-548d-808d-9bde7882c044" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445745}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335723 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a28c4186-c812-5117-83c3-943c9871c988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334419} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4057bb-935e-5406-ae84-0e99a9b2feff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334798} {:answer/id #uuid "d13828c4-d92d-5786-a8a2-c5df12308498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334819} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d2a3b1-1549-5cd5-ad78-61414e7f333c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334880} {:answer/id #uuid "f1aff7fe-125a-5328-8e93-9bb5ada34908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+4y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335109}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335109} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cbe8b04-b9bb-5077-ad66-1fa7004e8e05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "23f2ae10-1325-5e77-a3d0-434c6abe321a" :question/math-academy-id "q-64600" :question/problem "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=12\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c=9\\,\\text{kg/s},$ and an external force given by $F(t)=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{N}.$   \n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-9{y}^{′}-12y+15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+4y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "84c18301-1b51-5f16-bb6b-0040c628eb02" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445746}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445745 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3922d0-9e98-50fc-badf-c5493ce5c08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354795} {:answer/id #uuid "66a11be1-b8f6-5bf3-865a-32797a40b01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355576} {:answer/id #uuid "81aec1db-2243-5668-a3b3-f7f2049fd7a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355803} {:answer/id #uuid "9e22c7d3-b846-5397-9fb1-05bb1733229d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356074} {:answer/id #uuid "b95128ac-6579-590f-8e02-4fa489a727c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356287}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356287} :answer-field/id #uuid "03b4326e-a9f7-5be8-a7ee-fabfc0ce13c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cbe86c7f-4360-5aea-8ad0-c457c70df4e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94382" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(25t+ϕ)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$ It can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=25\\,⟹\\,\\omega =5.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =0.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nOnly III matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "b7e52d0a-b5e2-5243-aece-1bf9c6297d76"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445741 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "290d0431-34bf-5241-9afa-d9a916322c31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(\\pi )=({e}^{2\\pi }+1)\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445730} {:answer/id #uuid "36cbb06e-148b-54b4-91d4-a81f3e7aa5fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(\\pi )=({e}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{4})\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445731} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ef1d89-d277-5de1-9f67-b528494c18f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(\\pi )=(2{e}^{2\\pi }-\\frac{1}{4})\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445735} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb0d34a-111a-5f03-a468-2ba62a9a6c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(\\pi )=({e}^{\\pi }-1)\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445737} {:answer/id #uuid "ec44def9-9e39-53ed-aff9-0f399cc5b8fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(\\pi )=(2{e}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2})\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445738}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445737} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa0a7def-a246-5553-afa4-48288b187ed4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "071695a5-71a8-5be3-9519-3660055fc2db" :question/math-academy-id "q-94301" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=17{e}^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is an exponential force. The constant factor in the exponent is $1,$ which is not a root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular exponential solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)={e}^{t},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=-1,\\,B=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\pi -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi +{e}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =({e}^{\\pi }-1)\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d264594d-e99c-51f2-ad2d-ef5158a638af" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445743}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1944db5d-5dbb-5268-81cc-0ce9a0503edf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4.5y=3{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333219} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea66d73-08a4-526b-8c6d-aaa97b9b7f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4.5y=12{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333710} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa2a08d-f61a-5f63-8878-f2e9f1fa96ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4.5y=6{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334358} {:answer/id #uuid "d02a9da6-04d1-5120-b8a8-87bf90b8110c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9y=3{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334805} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0e4ecf-325b-59bd-90a8-d08faeba5d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9y=12{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335226}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333219} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b1b9c5a-ea8e-581e-b135-39bd76d3bc2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "07b06f33-b4ee-5f40-b29c-4546e0a946ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-64599" :question/problem "A particle of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ \nAn external force $F(t)=12{t}^{3}\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and  external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n4{y}^{″}=-18y+12{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4.5y=3{t}^{3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f76f52bb-0a3a-5b73-9f35-a1138cfc5f7b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445744}}] :activity/title "Forced Oscillators" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445728}]}
